2 _MARNM Oppenheim
1 NAME Rabbi David Oppenheim of Prague
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1664
2 ADDR
3 CITY Worms
3 STAE Rhineland-Palatinate
3 CTRY Germany
1 BURI
2 DATE SEP 1736
2 PLAC Jüdischer Friedhof
2 ADDR
3 CITY Prague
3 STAE Prague
3 CTRY Czechia
1 DEAT
2 DATE 23 SEP 1736
2 ADDR
3 CITY Prague
3 STAE Prague
3 CTRY Czechia
1 OCCU Rabbi
1 TITL R'
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} David was Rabbi of Prague. His famous collection of books was acquired by the University of Oxford in 1830.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.yivoencyclopedia.org/article.aspx/Oppenheim_David  '''David Oppenheim'''] (1664–1736), chief rabbi of Prague; bibliophile. Oppenheim was born in Worms, where his father, Abraham, was a communal leader. Samuel Oppenheim
2 CONC , financier and war contractor to Habsburg Emperor Leopold I, was his uncle. David Oppenheim’s teachers included, among others, Gershon (Ulif) Ashkenazi and Ya‘akov Sack (father of Tsevi Ashenazi, the Ḥakham Tsevi). In 1681 he mar
2 CONC ried Gnendl, daughter of the court Jew Leffmann Behrens (Lipmann Cohen) of Hanover.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1689 to 1702, Oppenheim served in Nikolsburg (Mikulov) as landesrabbiner, or chief rabbi, of Moravia, a post previously held by such prominent figures as Yehudah Leib ben Betsal’el (Maharal of Prague) and Yom Tov Lipmann Hell
2 CONC er. In 1701, Oppenheim was also named Nasi Erets Yisra’el and later rabbi of Jerusalem, no doubt in recognition of his financial support, and linking him to the Jewish population of the Land of Israel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oppenheim subsequently moved from Nikolsburg to Prague, where he took up the post of chief rabbi in 1702. His wife died in 1712, and he subsequently married Shifra, daughter of Binyamin Wolf Spira-Wedeles and widow of Yitsḥak Bond
2 CONC y, the leader of one of Jewish Prague’s political factions. With this marriage, he expanded his already substantial familial connections with the most influential circles of Prague Jewry. From 1713 on, he shared the duties of land
2 CONC esrabbiner of Bohemia, whose rabbinate was separate from that of Prague, with Spira; after Spira’s death in 1715, he held that position alone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oppenheim’s most significant legacy to posterity is his vast collection of Hebrew and Yiddish printed works and manuscripts, kept from 1703 at the home of his father-in-law in Hanover because of a justified fear of possible confis
2 CONC cation or destruction of Hebrew books in Prague. He acquired these books and manuscripts as gifts and by purchasing them directly from authors and owners—often heirs of deceased bibliophiles—throughout Europe, at commercial fairs,
2 CONC  and possibly through dealers. He prepared and, on at least one occasion, even published lists of desiderata. Today this collection constitutes a significant portion of the Judaica collection of Oxford University’s Bodleian Librar
2 CONC y.
2 CONT
2 CONT A wide range of works interested Oppenheim, from classical rabbinic texts to local ephemera. Hundreds of titles—particularly Yiddish compositions, including songs, “historical songs,” plays, stories, and legends—are known to us on
2 CONC ly through unica in his collection, indispensable to contemporary scholars’ understanding of seventeenth- and early eighteenth-century Yiddish literature. By the time of Oppenheim’s death, his library contained approximately 7,000
2 CONC  printed works and 1,000 manuscripts. It has formed the basis of the most significant bibliographies of Hebrew literature, including Moritz Steinschneider’s Catalogus librorum hebraeorum in Bibliotheca Bodleiana (1852–1882). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Steinschneider also published a separate bibliography of printed Yiddish books, pamphlets, and small ephemera, based on Oppenheim’s collection, in the journal Serapeum (Leipzig) in the years 1848–1849 (reprinted in 1961 as Jüdisch
2 CONC -Deutsche Literatur by the Yiddish Department of the Hebrew University in Jerusalem).
2 CONT
2 CONT Oppenheim wrote several rabbinic tracts, including Mo‘ed David on the Talmudic tractates Shabat and Betsah (1698); responsa, including the posthumously published volumes Nish’al David (1972–1982); and Seliḥot (1713). Most of his w
2 CONC ritings remain in manuscript; these include homiletic and theological works, additional Talmudic commentaries, correspondence, and catalogs of his library. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He also wrote introductions to Gershon Ashkenazi’s Sefer ‘avodat ha-Gershuni and Tif’eret ha-Gershuni and numerous approbations to rabbinic works. He supported the publication of other scholars’ works and published a few of the ma
2 CONC nuscripts he collected, such as the Torah commentary by Shemu’el ben Me’ir (Rashbam, grandson of Rashi), to which he wrote an introduction. In 1720, students in Oppenheim’s yeshiva published a purim-shpil in Yiddish whose title pa
2 CONC ge states that the work was performed publicly that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oppenheim’s family life, beginning with his first marriage, exemplifies the close interactions between rabbinic families and those of court Jews. His son, Joseph, married Telze, daughter of Samson Wertheimer, who was Samuel Oppenh
2 CONC eim’s nephew and his close associate in many court-related business affairs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughter Sarah married Ḥayim Yonah Te’omim-Fränkel (d. 1727), scion of a prominent rabbinic family and himself rabbi of Breslau. Sarah wrote a copy of Megilat Ester with her own hand; her father ruled in a rabbinic responsum t
2 CONC hat a megillah written by a woman was indeed fit for ritual use. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Oppenheim’s daughter Blumele married Aaron Ber Oppenheim of Frankfurt am Main; his daughter Jente married Phoebus, son of Seligman Cohen of Hanover; and his daughter Telze married Ber Cleve Gomperz.
2 CONT
2 CONT To what extent Oppenheim himself engaged in the financial activities with which these families are so closely identified, alongside his rabbinic career, is an open question in the scholarly literature. Some claim that he devoted h
2 CONC is time to business and to the enrichment of his own personal library at the expense of his rabbinic duties. These claims found their way into much subsequent writing about Oppenheim, but they have also been rejected by other scho
2 CONC lars. Much documentary evidence regarding Oppenheim’s life and activities remains untapped, and a full biography has yet to be written.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Suggested Reading'''
2 CONT
2 CONT # Charles Duschinsky, “Rabbi David Oppenheimer: Glimpses of His Life and Activity, Derived from His Manuscripts in the Bodleian Library,” Jewish Quarterly Review 20 (1929/30): 217–247; 
2 CONT #Tobias Jakobovits, “Das Prager und Böhmische Landesrabbinat, Ende des siebzehnten und Anfang des achtzehnten Jahrhunderts,” Jahrbuch der Gesellschaft für Geschichte der Juden in der Čechoslvakischen Republik 5 (1933): 79–136; 
2 CONT #Alexander Marx, “The History of David Oppenheimer’s Library,” in Studies in Jewish History and Booklore, pp. 238–255 (New York, 1944), see also pp. 213–219.
2 CONT
2 CONT >''Yivo Article Author: Rachel L. Greenblatt''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>><nowiki>-------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Oppenheim_ '''Rabbi David Oppenheim'''] (1664–1736) was the chief rabbi of Prague.  Oppenheim was born in 1664 in Worms, where his father, Abraham, was a communal leader. David Oppenheim's teach
2 CONC ers included Gershon Ashkenazi and Ya'akov Sack (father of Tsevi Ashenazi, the Hakham Tsevi). In 1681, he married Gnendl, daughter of the court Jew Leffmann Behrends (Lipmann Cohen) of Hanover.
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/view.jsp?artid=92&letter=O&search=david%20oppenheimer OPPENHEIM, DAVID BEN ABRAHAM] By : Executive Committee of the Editorial Board. M. Seligsohn 
2 CONT
2 CONT Austrian rabbi, cabalist, liturgist, mathematician, and bibliophile; born at Worms 1664; died at Prague Sept. 12, 1736. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After studying at Metz under Gershon Oulif, Oppenheim married Genendel, the daughter of Leffmann Behrends (Liepmann Cohen), court agent of Hanover. Through associations thus formed, combined with an immense fortune bequeathed to h
2 CONC im by his uncle, Samuel Oppenheim, court agent of Vienna, he became one of the leading Jews in Germany. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1691 Oppenheim was appointed rabbi of Mikulov (Nikolsburg), Moravia (Czech Republic) and chief rabbi of Moravia. In 1698 he accepted a call to the rabbinate of Brest-Litovsk, but continued to reside at Mikulov (Nikolsburg), Mor
2 CONC avia (Czech Republic) until 1702, when he became chief rabbi of Prague. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1713 Oppenheim was apappointed "Landesrabbiner" over one-half of Bohemia, and in 1718 over the whole of it. These nominations were confirmed by decrees of the emperors Leopold I., Joseph I., and Charles IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oppenheim, concurrently with his rabbinical duties, engaged in business transactions on a large scale, which necessitated his frequent absence from Prague. About this time a controversy arose between him and Jonathan Eybeschütz, t
2 CONC he rabbi of Prague. The latter, profiting by Oppenheim's frequent absences, tried continually to win the favor of the Jews of the city. As Oppenheim was a distinguished cabalist, he welcomed Nehemiah ?ayyun, the well-known Shabbet
2 CONC haian, whose erudition he admired, and gave him both moral and material aid. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Moreover, when, later, Oppenheim was drawn by the other rabbis into the fight against ?ayyun, he seemed to avoid any direct attack on the latter. In 1725 Oppenheim refused his signature to the excommunication of the Shabbethaians,
2 CONC  probably because Eybeschütz was one of the signatories. Toward the end of his life he became blind.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oppenheim was a prolific author, but of his works only the following have been published: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mo'ed Dawid,"
2 CONT
2 CONT novellæ, a part of which was printed with the 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bet Yehudah" of Judah b. Nissim (Dessau, 1698);
2 CONT
2 CONT novellæ on "Sugya Arba' Mittot" (Prague, 1725); 
2 CONT
2 CONT several responsa printed in the collections 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Shiyyure Keneset ha-Gedolah," 
2 CONT
2 CONT "?awwot Ya'ir," and others.
2 CONT
2 CONT The best known of his unpublished works are 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Me?om Dawid," a dictionary of all the places mentioned in the Talmud and of the events that occurred there; 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Me?udat ?iyyon," collectanea; 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Yad Dawid," commentary on the Pentateuch; 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Tefillah le-Dawid," homilies; 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Shelal Dawid," containing homilies, novellæ, and responsa; "
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Ir Dawid," collectanea and novellæ; 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Nish'al Dawid," responsa in the order of the four ?urim. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He left besides a large number of writings, containing novellæ on the Talmud and commentaries on many cabalistic works. In 1713 Oppenheim composed two seli?ot on the occasion of the epidemic which ravaged Prague at that time. In h
2 CONC is epitaph he is praised as a great mathematician.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oppenheim is especially renowned for his famous Hebrew library, the foundation of which was a numerous collection left to him by his uncle, Samuel Oppenheim, in which were some valuable manuscripts. Oppenheim labored energetically
2 CONC  to increase the library, and spared neither money nor time in purchasing rare and costly books. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1711 he compiled a list of books which he did not possess and made efforts to obtain them. Wolf ("Bibl. Hebr." i. 290) estimated the number of works in Oppenheim's possession at 7,000, including 1,000 manuscripts. Oppenheim was
2 CONC  desirous to throw open his library for public use, but could not do so at Prague on account of the censorship; he therefore removed it to Hanover, where it was thrown open under the protection of his father-in-law, Leffmann Behre
2 CONC nds, who, owing to his position, had great influence in that city. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Oppenheim's death the library came into the possession of his son Joseph Oppenheim, who pawned it with a senator of Hamburg for 50,000 marks ($12,000). In 1829 the collection, which had been stored in chests at Hamburg, was 
2 CONC bought by the Bodleian Library, Oxford, for 9,000 thalers (about $6,435). It consists of cabalistic, theological, Talmudic, philosophical, mathematical, and medical works. Catalogues of it have been made by Tychsen (Hanover, 1764)
2 CONC , Israel Bresslau (Hamburg, 1782), Isaac Metz, under the title "?ehillat Dawid" (ib. 1826), and Jacob Goldenthal (Leipsic, 1843). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The best-arranged catalogue is the "?ehillat Dawid." It contains 4,221 numbers, divided into four classes according to the sizes of the books, each class being subdivided into different branches, and each branch arranged in the al
2 CONC phabeticalorder of the titles. To the above-mentioned number of works must be added 1,200 bound with others. The manuscripts follow the printed books in each branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography: Hock-Lieben, Gal 'Ed (Hebr. part, No. 80; 
2 CONT
2 CONT German part, pp. 42 et seq.); 
2 CONT
2 CONT David Kaufmann, Samson Wertheimer, pp. 95 et seq.; 
2 CONT
2 CONT M. Wiener, in Berliner's Magazin, i. 27; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grätz, Gesch. 3d ed., x. 313 et seq., 325. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On Oppenheim's library: Zunz, Z. G. pp. 235 et seq.; 
2 CONT
2 CONT F. Lebrecht, in Orient, Lit. v. 247; 
2 CONT
2 CONT L. Dukes, ib. xi. 250, 262, 297; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hartmann, in Yedidyah, vi., Berlin, 1820-21.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Abraham OPPENHEIMER b: 1633 in Worms, Deutschland (Germany) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Bluemle (Rechle) WOHL b: ABT 1638
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Genendel BEHRENDS b: ABT 1664 in Hannover (Hanover), Deutschland (Germany)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: ABT 1681
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT  Joseph ben David OPPENHEIMER b: ABT 1683
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sarah OPPENHEIMER b: 1695
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jente OPPENHEIMER b: 1696
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 2 Shifrah SPIRA b: ABT 1671 in Praha (Prague), Bohemia (Czech Republic)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: ABT 1714
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: The Prague Jewish Community in the Late 17th and Early 18th Centuries
2 CONT
2 CONT Author: Alexandr Putík
2 CONT
2 CONT Publication: In 'Judaica Bohemiae' Issue no. XXXV /1999, Státni Zidovské Muzeum v Praze / National Jewish Museum, Prague, 1999
2 CONT
2 CONT Repository: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: http://www.ceeol.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Media: Book
2 CONT
2 CONT Page: p. 93
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: The Jewish Quarterly Review
2 CONT
2 CONT Author: Dropsie University, Dropsie College for Hebrew and Cognate Learning
2 CONT
