1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Judah ben Isaac Messer Leon (1166–1224) was a French tosafist born in Paris. According to Gross he was probably a descendant of Rashi, and a pupil of Isaac ben Samuel of Dampierre and his son Elhanan. He married a 
2 CONC daughter of Abraham ben Joseph of Orleans, who has been identified by Jacobs (Jews of Angevin England, p. 409) with Abraham fil Rabbi Joce, chief Jew in London in 1186. In a list of that year associated with Abraham occurs the nam
2 CONC e of Leo Blund, whom Jacobs identifies with Judah ben Isaac (ib. p. 88; comp. Bacher, in J.Q.R. vi.360).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Leon must have left Paris in 1182, when all Jews were expelled from the French king's dominions; he did not return till 1198. According to Gross, however, he received his chief training at Dampierre under Samson of Sens, Simso
2 CONC n of Coucy, Solomon of Dreux, and Abraham ben Nathan of Lunel. Shortly after 1198 he returned to Paris and founded an important school of tosafists, in which were trained, among others, Jehiel ben Joseph (Sir Leon's successor), Is
2 CONC aac ben Moses of Vienna (author of Or Zarua), Samuel ben Solomon (Sir Morel of Falaise), and Moses of Coucy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He appears to have composed tosafot to most of the tractates of the Talmud, traces being found of his annotations to twenty tractates. The only collection that has been published are his additamenta to Berakot, published at Warsaw
2 CONC  in 1863. A long fragment of his tosafot to Abodah Zarah is still extant in a manuscript that formerly belonged to Samuel David Luzzatto and Solomon Joachim Halberstam (R.E.J. vii.55) and that is now in the possession of Jews' Col
2 CONC lege, London. A few of his responsa are also found, chiefly in various additions to the Mordecai, while reference is also found to his commentary on the Pentateuch, in which he appears to have followed the method of Rashbam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judah wrote several poems — an Aramaic description of the Decalogue (Zunz, Ritus, p. 198), a pizmon (idem, Literaturgesch. p. 329), and a piyyut (Landshuth, Ammude ha-'Abodah, i.68). He is not, however, to be identified with the m
2 CONC ystical Judah Ḥasid, to whom are attributed the Sefer ha-Ḥasidim and an ethical will. Among the writers whom Judah quotes may be mentioned Amram Gaon, Sherira Gaon, Hai Gaon, and Nissim Gaon, Alfasi, Maimonides, Elijah ben Menahem
2 CONC , Gershom ben Judah, Jacob of Orleans, Jacob of Corbeil, Joseph Kara, Joseph Bekor Shor, Yom-Tov of Joigny, and Rashi.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Paris in 1224 (Solomon Luria, Responsa, No. 29).
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeremy Richard Lichtman (Nov 7, 2025)
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is based on a) Artscroll "The Rishonim" and b) Wikipedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wiki here: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judah_ben_Isaac_Messer_Leon
2 CONT Says need to be careful not to confuse with Yehuda ben Yechiel.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Birth date given as 1166 in Paris.
2 CONT 2. His wife's name is conjectural (see Wikipedia), but I think makes sense.
2 CONT 3. I don't see any children listed.
2 CONT 4. His father's profile is incorrect. I'll put notes there. Atrscroll says that his father, Isaac, was descended from R Yehuda ben Nassan, and also that Isaac's sister was the wife of the RI. This solves the mystery of the wife of
2 CONC  Yehuda ben Yom Tov of Falaise, R"I haZaken - The Elder
