1 NOTE {geni:about_me} אלף מרגליות, ערך 230
2 CONT נולד בפולין, ולכן נקרא פולק, עבר לפראג ושם כיהן כראב"ד ור"מ פראג..לאחר מחלוקת עם רבנים אחרים כאשר הגן על סולקא (אחות אשתו),עזב את פראג ועבר להתגורר בקראקא ועשאה מרכז לתורה "כרכא דחולא בה״. לא נשא פנים לאיש בגבורתה של תורה, ולא חס 
2 CONC על רופא שטימא יהודיה, בשל כך הלשינו עליו וניאלץ לפרוש וברח לא"י לעשר שנים.
2 CONT
2 CONT ״בעל צמח תורה כותב עליו: "יחיד בדורו בחריפותו בקיאותו וחידוד שכלו, ושמעו הלך מסוף העולם עד סופו.
2 CONT ״גדולי הדור העריצו אותו וכינו אותו ״הצבי ישראל  ראש גולת אריאל
2 CONT
2 CONT נפטר ביום כ"ג  סיון ש"א ,נקבר בלובלין, נוסח מצבתו נשמר ובו כתוב שהוא נקרא גם ר' קופלמן הלוי..
2 CONT
2 CONT חמותו הייתה מרת רחל זוגתו השניה של ר' משה פישל מקראקא, שהיה שר המכס בפולין 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Chaim Shulman wrote:
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Rabbi Wein in Herald of Destiny, Rabbi Yaakov Pollak (1455-1530) was the first famous Polish-born Talmudic scholar and rabbinic leader. He traveled to Germany to study at the yeshivah in Nuremberg and later served as 
2 CONC a rabbi in Prague. He was there embroiled in a bitter halachic dispute with the rabbis of Padua, Italy, regarding his attempt to annul a marriage without the necessity for a divorce...Searching for peace, Rabbi Yaakov left Prague 
2 CONC and returned to Poland at the end of the fifteenth century, where he found great acceptance and honor. He served as rabbi in Cracow and later in Lublin. He established a large yeshivah and great numbers of Polish Jewish young men 
2 CONC flocked to his lectures, wherein Rabbi Pollak developed a most unique and intricate system of pilpul. This methodology, which became very popular in Poland, proved ... controversial... in later generations, this methodology was gr
2 CONC eatly modified and brought more in line with [standard] Talmudic study norms the world over. However, pilpul, no matter how restricted and refined, forever remained part of Polish Jewish life and Torah study. It was the pepper tha
2 CONC t sharpened the speech, ideas and creative instinct of Polish Jewry. Rabbi Yaakov Pollak, the champion of unrestricted pilpul, is considered the father of Torah scholarship and rabbinic authority in Poland. He left no works in pri
2 CONC nt, but we can positively state that his influence in Poland was strong and lasting and that those who studied under him did so with love and reverence. The main disciple of Rabbi Yaakov Pollak was Rabbi Shalom Shachna of Lublin. 
2 CONC He headed a famous yeshivah in Lublin for over forty years and almost all of the great rabbinic leaders of Jewish Poland in the sixteenth century were his students. Rabbi Shalom Shachna, like his master, left no written works, but
2 CONC  is known to have continued the intense use of pilpul in his lectures. He was world renowned for his scholarship and genius. It was during his lifetime that Jewish Poland began to gain recognition as a center of Torah and rabbinic
2 CONC  authority for Ashkenazim.
