1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Judah_Katzenellenbogen '''Samuel Judah Katzenellenbogen'''] (1521 in Padua, Italy – March 25, 1597 in Padua) was an Italian Rabbi, the son of Rabbi Meir ben Isaac Katzenellenbog
2 CONC en).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was distinguished even in his youth for his scholarship and his oratorical gifts. He was associated, both as rabbi and as writer, with his father, after whose death, in 1565, he was elected rabbi at Venice.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had many pupils, one of whom had a bust made of his beloved teacher, who was of imposing appearance, and set it up in the schoolhouse so as to have it constantly before his eyes (Eisenstadt-Wiener, Da'at Kedoshim, p. 82, St. Pe
2 CONC tersburg, 1897-98). Chaim Joseph David Azulai ("Shem ha-Gedolim," s.v.) speaks of this fact in connection with Samuel Judah Katzenellenbogen's great-grandfather, Judah Minz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katzenellenbogen was highly respected by his coreligionists throughout Italy, and, because of his wide scholarship, was honored by Joseph Karo, Solomon Luria, and Moses Isserles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Non-Jews also esteemed him highly: Paul Weidner, a converted Jew, dedicated his works to him. He is said to have accorded a hospitable reception to the Polish prince Nicholas Radziwill when the latter visited Padua. When the princ
2 CONC e found himself in need of money he appealed to Katzenellenbogen, who lent him the necessary funds for continuing his journey, requesting in return that he deal leniently with the Jews in his country, and protect them against the 
2 CONC accusation of ritual murder.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Katzenellenbogen's death Leo Modena delivered the funeral oration, which has been printed in the Mivchar Yehudah (p. 63b).
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel left one son, who is known under the name of Saul Wahl. Katzenellenbogen is the author of several responsa, which are included in the responsa collection of Moses Isserles (Nos. 23, 126, 127, 129) and in that of Samuel Kala
2 CONC 'i; and of twelve derashot, published by his pupil (Venice, 1594; reprinted Lemberg, 1811, where the author's name is erroneously given as J. Minz).
2 CONT
2 CONT Katzenellenbogen also contributed some of the notes to the annotated edition of Maimonides' Yad ha-HazaKah (Venice, 1550), which notes Azulai (Shem ha-Gedolim, s.v.) ascribes to Meir of Padua.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1546 was a Dayan (judge) in Venice, in 1566 chief rabbi of Venice.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Source=
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography:
2 CONT *Kerem Hemed iii. 94 et seq.;
2 CONT *Steinschneider, Cat. Bodl. No. 7047
2 CONT *Furst, Bibl. Jud. ii 179;
2 CONT *Zunz, Monatstage des Kalenderjahres, p. 15
2 CONT *[http://chfreedman.blogspot.com/search/label/Death%20In%20Venice%20-%20%20Seeking%20the%20Katzenellenbogen%20tombstones Death In Venice - seeking the Katzenellenbogen tombstones by Chaim Freedman, July 2008.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alt: Shmuel Yehuda Katzenellnbogen
2 CONT
2 CONT In his youth, a distinguished scholar & orator. Studied under his father. In 1565, elected Rabbi of Venice, at the age of 45. Wrote numerous responsa and sermons published in the responsa of R. Moses Isserles and R. Samuel Kalai. 
2 CONC He also contributed some notes to the annotated editions of Maimonides' Yad Hachazakah (Venice 1550). Highly esteemed by Jews and non-Jews.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his father's death, he was elected rabbi in Venice. One of his students had made a bust of him and placed it in the yeshiva. R. Shmuel was respected by R. Joseph Caro, R. Solomon Luria, and R. Moses Isserles.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Prince of Poland, Prince Radziwill found himself in need of money during a trip through Padua, R. Shmuel lent him the necessary funds for continuing his journey. This debt was repaid when an urgent need to fill the positi
2 CONC on of king of Poland for one night during elections. R' Shmuel's son, R' Shaul, was entrusted this honor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.maxpages.com/nodabyehuda/Samuel_J_Katzenellenbogen_1521
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi - Principal of the Vinitzer (?) Yeshiva
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as Mari Mine. Rabbi in Venice then in Packa.  Died in 1597.  Because of his accomplishments in Talmudic learning he was held in high esteem.  Famous for his 12 sermons 'deraschot' (see M.Wahl).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Rabbi Shmuel Yehuda Katzenellenbogen, Av Beit Din of Vinnitsa
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Judah Katzenellenbogen
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Judah_Katzenellenbogen
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Judah Katzenellenbogen Personal details Born 	1521 Padua, Republic of Venice Died 	25 March 1597 (aged 75–76) Padua, Republic of Venice Father 	Meir Katzenellenbogen
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Judah Katzenellenbogen (1521 – 25 March 1597) was an Italian Rabbi, the son of Rabbi Meir Katzenellenbogen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was distinguished even in his youth for his scholarship and his oratorical gifts. He was associated, both as rabbi and as writer, with his father, after whose death, in 1565, he was elected rabbi at Venice.[1] He had many pupil
2 CONC s, one of whom had a bust made of his beloved teacher, who was of imposing appearance, and set it up in the schoolhouse so as to have it constantly before his eyes (Eisenstadt-Wiener, Da'at Kedoshim, p. 82, St. Petersburg, 1897–98
2 CONC ). Chaim Yosef David Azulai ("Shem ha-Gedolim," s.v.) speaks of this fact in connection with Samuel Judah Katzenellenbogen's great-grandfather, Judah Minz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katzenellenbogen was highly respected by his coreligionists throughout Italy, and, because of his wide scholarship, was honored by Joseph Karo, Solomon Luria, and Moses Isserles. Non-Jews also esteemed him highly: Paul Weidner, a 
2 CONC converted Jew, dedicated his works to him. He is said to have accorded a hospitable reception to the Polish prince Mikołaj Krzysztof "the Orphan" Radziwiłł when the latter visited Padua. When the prince found himself in need of mo
2 CONC ney he appealed to Katzenellenbogen, who lent him the necessary funds for continuing his journey, requesting in return that he deal leniently with the Jews in his country, and protect them against the accusation of ritual murder.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Katzenellenbogen's death Leo Modena delivered the funeral oration, which has been printed in the Mivchar Yehudah (p. 63b). Samuel left one son, who is known under the name of Saul Wahl. Katzenellenbogen is the author of several
2 CONC  responsa, which are included in the responsa collection of Moses Isserles (Nos. 23, 126, 127, 129) and in that of Samuel Kala'i; and of twelve derashot, published by his pupil (Venice, 1594; reprinted Lemberg, 1811, where the aut
2 CONC hor's name is erroneously given as J. Minz). Katzenellenbogen also contributed some of the notes to the annotated edition of Maimonides' Yad ha-HazaKah (Venice, 1550), which notes Azulai (Shem ha-Gedolim, s.v.) ascribes to Meir of
2 CONC  Padua. Jewish Encyclopedia bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT     Kerem Hemed, iii. 94 et seq.;
2 CONT     Steinschneider, Cat. Bodl. No. 7047;
2 CONT     Edelmann, Gedullat Sha'ul, pp. xiii. (where Katzenellenbogen's epitaph is quoted), 2b, 29 et seq., London, 1854;
2 CONT     Julius Fürst, Bibl. Jud. ii. 179;
2 CONT     Zunz, Monatstage des Kalenderjahres, p. 15;
2 CONT     S. Kahan, 'Anaf 'Eẓ Abot, p. 31, Cracow, 1903;
2 CONT     Eisenstadt-Wiener, Da'at Ḳedoshim, pp. 82 et seq.D. M. K.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     Karpeles, Gustav (2009). Jewish Literature, and Other Essays. General Books. ISBN 978-1-150-67535-5.
2 CONT
2 CONT  This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: Singer, Isidore; et al., eds. (1901–1906). "Katzenellenbogen". Jewish Encyclopedia. New York: Funk & Wagnalls Company.
2 CONT --------------------
