1 NOTE Samuel Judah Katzenellenbogen:
2 CONT http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/view.jsp?artid=135&letter=K&search=Meïr%20Katzenellenbogen.#419
2 CONT
2 CONT Italian rabbi; born in 1521 at Padua; died there March 25, 1597; son of R. Meïr b. Isaac of Padua (Meïr Padua). He was distinguished even in his youth for his scholarship and his oratorical gifts. He was associated, both as rabb
2 CONC i and as writer, with his father, after whose death, in 1565, he was elected rabbi at Venice. He had many pupils, one of whom had a bust made of his beloved teacher, who was of imposing appearance, and set it up in the schoolhouse
2 CONC  so as to have it constantly before his eyes (Eisenstadt-Wiener, "Da'at ?edoshim," p. 82, St. Petersburg, 1897-98). Azulai ("Shem ha-Gedolim," s.v.) speaks of this fact in connection with Samuel Judah Katzenellenbogen's great-gran
2 CONC dfather, Judah Minz.
2 CONT Katzenellenbogen was highly respected by his coreligionists throughout Italy, and, because of his wide scholarship, was honored by Joseph Caro, Solomon Luria, and Moses Isserles. Non-Jews also esteemed him highly: Paul Weidner, a 
2 CONC converted Jew, dedicated his works to him. He is said to have accorded a hospitable reception to the Polish prince Radziwill when the latter visited Padua. When the prince found himself in need of money he appealed to Katzenellenb
2 CONC ogen, who lent him the necessary funds for continuing his journey, requesting in return that he deal leniently with the Jews in his country, and protect them against the accusation of ritual murder.
2 CONT On Katzenellenbogen's death Leo Modena delivered the funeral oration, which has been printed in the "Mib?ar Yehudah" (p. 63b). Samuel left one son, who is known under the name of Saul Wahl. Katzenellenbogen is the author of severa
2 CONC l responsa, which are included in the responsa collection of Moses Isserles (Nos. 23, 126, 127, 129) and in that of Samuel ?ala'i; and of twelve derashot, published by his pupil (Venice, 1594; reprinted Lemberg, 1811, where the au
2 CONC thor's name is erroneously given as J. Minz). Katzenellenbogen also contributed some of the notes to the annotated edition of Maimonides' "Yad ha-?aza?ah" (Venice, 1550), which notes Azulai ("Shem ha-Gedolim," s.v.) ascribes to Me
2 CONC ïr of Padua.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography: Kerem ?emed, iii. 94 et seq.;
2 CONT Steinschneider, Cat. Bodl. No. 7047;
2 CONT Edelmann, Gedullat Sha'ul, pp. xiii. (where Katzenellenbogen's epitaph is quoted), 2b, 29 et seq., London, 1854;
2 CONT Fürst, Bibl. Jud. ii. 179;
2 CONT Zunz, Monatstage des Kalenderjahres, p. 15;
2 CONT S. Kahan, 'Anaf 'E? Abot, p. 31, Cracow, 1903;
2 CONT Eisenstadt-Wiener, Da'at ?edoshim, pp. 82 et seq.
