1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Abraham is the only child who remained in Witzenhausen. According to an eighteenth-century genealogy Abraham Witzenhausen was an ordained rabbi (Kaufmann 1896, p. 297). A list of Jews from 1622 mentions “Abraham Sc
2 CONC hmueln Sohn” in Witzenhausen (StA Marburg, Best. 40a XVI Gen. Nr. 1). In 1625, “Abraham Goldschmidt” paid Schutzgeld in Witzenhausen (StA Marburg, AR II Witzenhausen 10, Jg. 1625). A list from 1626 simply calls him Abraham (StA Ma
2 CONC rburg, Best. 40a XVI Gen. Nr. 1). He is the only Abraham on all three lists. In 1636, he signed a document in German as “Abraham Judt fun Wittzenhaussen” (StA Marburg, 17 d von Uslar, #37; see Kröger (2011), p. 124). In 1664, Abra
2 CONC ham signed a document as a representative of the Jewish community of Hesse. Cohen (1996, p. 441) has reconstructed his damaged Hebrew signature as follows (titles omitted): Avraham ben Baruch Daniel Shmuel Segal (אברהם [בכ]הרר ב[ר
2 CONC ו]ך דניאל שמואל [סג"]ל). He was buried in Witzenhausen on April 9, 1676. His tombstone has not been preserved. However, the German records in Witzenhausen mention the date of his burial: "Am 9t(en) Aprilis ist Abraham Goldtschmidt
2 CONC  begraben worden” (StA Marburg, AR II Witzenhausen 10, Jg. 1676).
2 CONT
2 CONT References: Daniel J. Cohen, Die Landjudenschaften in Deutschland als Organe Jüdische Selbstverwaltung von der Frühen Neuzeit bis ins Neunzehnte Jahrhundert: Ein Quellensammlung, Band 1 (Jerusalem: Israeli Academy of Sciences, 199
2 CONC 6). David Kaufmann, Aus Heinrich Heine’s Ahnensaal (Breslau: S. Schottlaender, 1896). Rüdiger Kröger, “Deutschsprachige Literalität in Familie un Umfeld von Jobst Goldschmidt alias Josef Hameln,” pp 119-137 in Birgit E. Klein and 
2 CONC Rotraud Ries (eds.), Selbstzeugnisse und Ego-Dokumente frühneuzeitlicher Juden in Aschkenas: Beispiel, Methoden und Konzepte (Metropol, 2011).
