1 NAME Elhanan Elkan /Frenkel/
2 GIVN Elhanan Elkan
2 SURN Frenkel
2 _MARNM Fraenkel
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Judenfriedhof
2 ADDR
3 CITY Georgensgmünd
3 STAE BY
3 CTRY Germany
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1654
2 ADDR
3 CITY Vienna
3 STAE Vienna
3 CTRY Austria
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1720
2 PLAC Prison
2 ADDR
3 CITY Würzburg
3 STAE BY
3 POST 97
3 CTRY Germany
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elkan was the son of Rabbi Enoch Levi, who had married Ritsche, daughter of the Viennese banker Jakob Koppel, in a favorable match, and belonged to Vienna's Jewish elite. After being expelled in 1670, he moved to F
2 CONC ürth, where many Jews already lived. There, two feuding factions existed: a minority favored the Margrave of Ansbach, while the majority supported the Bishop of Bamberg. Fränkel profited from this conflict when he sided with the H
2 CONC ouse of Ansbach in 1703. He presented himself to the new Margrave Wilhelm Friedrich to better control the community in the Margrave's interest.
2 CONT
2 CONT In return, Fränkel became Barnoss (from the Hebrew word Parnas), leader of the Ansbach faction, in 1704, and Oberbarnoss of the entire community in 1708. He rose to become the Margrave's comprehensive financial advisor and court f
2 CONC actor. This created many enemies for him among both Jews and Christians.  His younger brother, Rabbi Zwi Hirsch (1662–1739?) from Fürth, simultaneously became Chief Rabbi of the Margraviate of Ansbach in 1708. There, Elkan ousted 
2 CONC the court Jew Marx Model in 1708 with accusations of embezzlement, and Model's family sought revenge.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 1712, Elkan and Zwi Hirsch Fränkel were arrested following a denunciation [https://www.geni.com/people/Friedrich-Christold/6000000013632101521 by a convert, his first cousin, Ischay Fränkel.](On Geni) Elkan was accused
2 CONC  of possessing blasphemous books, influencing the court politically, and embezzling public funds. Zwi Hirsch was alleged to have written a book on witchcraft. A personal enemy headed the investigating commission. Elkan was taken i
2 CONC nto custody and placed in solitary confinement. Despite a lack of evidence, the accusations were declared true. The Margrave did not protect him but instead sentenced him to flogging, life imprisonment, confiscation of his propert
2 CONC y, and the expulsion of his family.  Rabbi Zwi Hirsch also remained imprisoned until 1723 and died around 1739 in Georgensgmünd, where a Jewish community with a synagogue existed.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 2, 1712, a large crowd gathered in the marketplace to witness the public punishment. After his back was exposed, he received nine lashes, then was taken to Wülzburg Fortress. He died there in chains in 1720.
