1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Abraham ben Judah ha-Levi Minz was an Italian rabbi who flourished at Padua in the first half of the 16th century, father-in-law of Meïr Katzenellenbogen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Minz" family of rabbis and scholars, derive their name from the town of Mayence and founded in the fifteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Minz studied chiefly under his father, Judah Minz, whom he succeeded as rabbi and head of the yeshiva of Padua. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Ibn Yaḥya (Shalshelet ha-Ḳabbalah, p. 51a, Amsterdam, 1697), it was with Abraham Minz that Jacob Polak had the quarrel which ended in their excommunicating each other; according to most other authorities, the quarrel 
2 CONC was with Judah Minz. Ibn Yaḥya further says that the Italian rabbis believe that Polak and Abraham Minz died on the same day (according to David Gans in 1530; according to Halberstam in 1541). Minz was the author of a number of de
2 CONC cisions that were printed with those of R. Lewa of Ferrara (Venice, 1511). He was the author also of Seder Giṭṭin wa-Ḥaliẓah, a treatise on divorce and ḥaliẓah, printed with the responsa of his father and of his son-in-law (ib. 15
2 CONC 53).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT -------------------- He inherited the high qualities of his father and followed him as Rabbi and leader of his house of learning in Padua.  (xii.42).
2 CONT
