1 CONT G5.4. Nafatli Hirsch, Shtadlan or Jewish Representative to the Lithuanian Government. He lived in Frankfurt-on-Main and came to Vienna in 1707, in  an attempt to improve the Jewish situation there. He died in Vienna on the  4th of
1 CONC  Iyar.
0 @N6000000009752722651@ NOTE Rabbi in France
0 @N6000000003495148289@ NOTE
1 CONT aka Maharik Morenu Ha Rav 
1 CONT MaHaRIK, RIK
1 CONT Rabbi Joseph Kolon (Colon)
1 CONT
1 CONT French-Jewish descent. Lived 1469 in Mestre, near Venezia.
1 CONT
1 CONT from Jewish Encyclopedia:
1 CONT The foremost Talmudist of Italy in the second half of the fifteenth century; born probably at Chambéry, Savoy, about 1420; died at Pavia 1480. Colon (whose name is probably identical with the French "colombe," dove) belonged to th
1 CONC e scholarly family of the Trabots, who emigrated from France to Italy in the fifteenth century. The teachers of the boy were his father—himself an eminent Talmudist—and a certain Mordecai b. Nathan. Colon left his home at an early
1 CONC  age—not, however, as Grätz says ("Gesch." 3d ed., viii. 253), in consequence of the expulsion of the Jews from Savoy, which took place in 1471. For a time he led a wandering life, and was forced to gain his living by teaching chi
1 CONC ldren. [FULL ARTICLE AT WEBSITE]
1 CONT
1 CONT from Wikipedia:
1 CONT
1 CONT Early years
1 CONT
1 CONT Colon (whose name is related to the French word colombe, or 'dove') was a scion of the Trabotto family, which was known for its large number of scholars. After the final expulsion of Jews from the French Kingdom in 1394, his famil
1 CONC y emigrated first to the Franche-Comte and subsequently settled in the city of Chambéry, the capital of the Duchy of Savoy, which was home to a significant population of rabbinic scholars. Among these were Yohanan Treves, the last
1 CONC  chief rabbi of France and Jacob HaLevi, renowned as Maharil Jacob ben Moses Möln.
1 CONT
1 CONT The exact year and place of Joseph Colon's birth cannot be determined, but is estimated to be at the beginning of the 1420s in Chambéry, a city whose Jewish population was overwhelmigly made up of individuals of French, rather tha
1 CONC n German, origin. It was within this ambience that the young Joseph Colon received his Talmudic education, which was heavily imbued with the style, traditions and Talmudic methodology of medieval French Jewry. Chiefly, he studied 
1 CONC under the tutelage of his father, Solomon Trabotto, a noted Talmudist and Kabbalist, though he does refer to others as his teachers, and recalls participating in learned discussion with other local scholars. Colon left Chambéry in
1 CONC  the early 1450s and settled in the Italian Piedmont, which had become part of the Duchy of Savoy. This move was the result of a combination of new opportunities on the other side of the Alps, combined with increasing anti-Judaism
1 CONC  in Trans-Alpine Savoy. It was not, however, as Grätz claims ("Gesch." 3d ed., viii. 253), a consequence of the expulsion of the Jews from Savoy, which only occurred in 1471. For a time he led a wandering life, and was forced to g
1 CONC ain his living by teaching children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Travels and growing fame as scholar
1 CONT
1 CONT About 1469 Colon officiated as rabbi in Pieve de Sacco, in Venetian territory and continued on to Mestre, near Venice. Subsequently he was rabbi at Bologna and Mantua and, according to a report in Gedaliah Ibn Yahya's Shalshelet h
1 CONC a-Qabbalah, became embroiled in a quarrel with Rabbi Judah Messer Leon, both being banished by the authorities. Thereupon he relocated to Pavia. At the same time Colon's decisions in civil as well as religious questions were sough
1 CONC t from far and wide-from German cities, such as Ulm and Nuremberg, as well as from Constantinople. He wrote a commentary on the Pentateuch, and novellæ on the Talmud and on the legal codex of Moses of Coucy, the Sefer Mitzvot Gado
1 CONC l. His major legacy were, however, his responsa. These were collected after his death by his son-in-law Rabbi Gershon Treves, and by one of his pupils, Hiyya Meïr ben David and were published in Venice in 1519 by Daniel Bomberg. T
1 CONC hey were subsequently republished many times. In 1984, E. D. Pines published fifty new responsa from manuscript. Many more of his responsa remain unpublished.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Responsa
1 CONT
1 CONT Colon's responsa are among the classic productions in this field of rabbinic literature and exercised tremendous influence on the subsequent development of Jewish Law or Halakhah. His decisions had massive influence upon all subse
1 CONC quent legal development. His influence is particularly notable in the Ashkenazic orbit, as reflected in Moses Isserles' glosses on the Shulhan Arukh. Colon's responsa were the central pillar of later Italian halakhah, and there is
1 CONC  scarcely an Italian rabbi of the 16th, 17th and 18th century who does not quote him. These responsa are distinguished by his encyclopedic knowledge and methodical analysis of sources. He attempts to identify the basic principles 
1 CONC underlying his sources and to elucidate the conceptual framework within which he renders his rulings. His legal method also resembles the mode of analysis known as pilpul. Established custom (or minhag) played a unique place in hi
1 CONC s thinking and he defines its authority. In this context, he served as the defender of a uniquely French school of Ashkenazic Law and Lore. The Mishneh Torah of Maimonides enjoys a preeminent place in his writings. His extensive c
1 CONC omments thereupon, scattered throughout his responsa and lecture notes, helped to set the agenda for later scholars. Colon's responsa are marked by tremendous deference to authorities of the past. Hesitating to decide between them
1 CONC , he resorted to methods of legal determination which removed or minimized this necessity (e.g. Halakha k'Bathra'i).
1 CONT
1 CONT Colon's self-confidence is remarkable. He had a strong regard for right and justice. Firmly, though respectfully, he reproved Rabbi Israel Bruna, the foremost German talmudist of his time, for overstepping the bounds of his author
1 CONC ity. Responsum No. 4, addressed to the congregation of Regensburg, is highly important. A number of Jews of that community having been falsely accused, and a sum of money having to be raised for their ransom, the surrounding place
1 CONC s and neighboring communities refused to contribute, at least insofar as it was a question of paying a fixed tax instead of making voluntary contributions. Colon decided that the communities in question could not refuse to pay the
1 CONC ir share, since the same false accusation might be made against them also, and if the accused in this case were proved innocent and ransomed, they would then be safe from danger.
1 CONT
1 CONT Trabotto died in Padua at the age of about sixty. Most references agree on his year of death, although one lists it as 1484, four years later than generally accepted.
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Joseph_Colon_Trabotto&oldid=179050088
1 CONT
1 CONT from his Responsa:
1 CONT Question: 
1 CONT Do parental objections to a child's choice of marital partner carry any halakhic weight?
1 CONT
1 CONT Response
1 CONT There are three reasons why, if the match is to an appropriate person, that the parent's objections need not be heeded.
1 CONT 1. A child is not obligated to suffer the pain that not marrying someone fitting in order to honor his parents. The Gemara discusses who is responsible for footing the bill for parental honor and concludes that the son is not resp
1 CONC onsible to spend his own money. A child may feed, clothe, and care for the parent using the parent's own money. If a child does not have to expend money, he certainly does not have to go through a painful marriage to honor his par
1 CONC ents.
1 CONT 2. A parent forbidding a child to marry a desired mate is tantamount to telling him to transgress a mitzva. The Talmud says (beginning of the second chapter of Kiddushin) that a man may not betrothe a woman before seeing her, lest
1 CONC  he not be attracted to her. This is based on, "Love your neighbor as yourself." Other rabbinic sources also emphasize the importance of marital love. A father who forbids his son to marry the bride of his choice is in effect tell
1 CONC ing him not to observe "Love your neighbor as yourself." The Gemara in Yevamot 5 rules that since both parent and child are obligated to honor G-d, if parental honor entails transgressing a positive or negative mitzva the child ma
1 CONC y not follow the parental command. The Rosh (Teshuvot HaRosh 15:5) applied this rule to a father interested in his son prolonging a feud. The son, who was interested in reconciliation, was told that in this instance he must ignore
1 CONC  the parental command for it contradicts the Divine one.
1 CONT 3. The Torah commands honor and reverence of parents, but does not legislate parental authority. Honor and reverence are limited to acts that benefit the parent. The Gemara speaks of honor as feeding, clothing and attending to the
1 CONC  parent's other needs; reverence means not contradicting him, not standing in his special place, not detracting from his status. However, the mitzva is not a blanket obligation for a child to obey all of his parent's commands. Det
1 CONC ermining whom the child marries is outside the scope of parental authority. [The Sefer Hamakneh holds that a child is obligated to fulfill any parental commands as long as they do not contradict Torah or Rabbinic ones. A number of
1 CONC  Rishonim seem to go against this approach.]
1 CONT
1 CONT grandchildren could say Kaddish for grandparents provided the parents did not object38.
